  {
    "id": "articles-gender",
    "title": "Articles & Noun Gender (el/la, un/una)",
    "text": "Every Spanish noun is masculine or feminine. Learn the article with the word.\n\nMasculine: **el** libro, **un** libro (mostly words ending in **-o**)\nFeminine: **la** casa, **una** casa (mostly words ending in **-a**)\n\n**Exceptions to watch:**\n- *el día*, *el mapa*, *el problema*, *el tema* (masculine despite -a)\n- *la mano*, *la foto*, *la moto* (feminine despite -o)\n\nEndings that are usually masculine: -or, -aje, -ma (Greek origin).\nEndings usually feminine: -dad, -tad, -ción, -sión, -umbre.",
    "table": [
      [
        "",
        "Masculine",
        "Feminine"
      ],
      [
        "singular",
        "el libro / un libro",
        "la casa / una casa"
      ],
      [
        "plural",
        "los libros / unos libros",
        "las casas / unas casas"
      ],
      [
        "typical ending",
        "-o, -or, -aje",
        "-a, -dad, -ción"
      ]
    ],
    "exercises": [
